\item \textbf{Forecast and realization.} We evaluate the unadjusted back-transformed variance forecast $\hat\sigma^2_t$ defined in Section~\ref{sec:rh_methods_setup} against the actual, unwinsorized realized variance of the closing interval, $\RV_t \equiv \RV_{t,c}$ (winsorization is applied to $\tilde y_{t,c}$ during model estimation only).
  % 2026-10-15 (user): parked -- "Out of sample period doesn't make sense for forecasts because we're performing
  % rolling backtests ..."; T_oos keeps its symbol, redefined as the forecast origins of the rolling exercise. Was:
  % "The out-of-sample period is the set $\mathcal T_{\mathrm{oos}}$ of trading days on which the forecasts are
  % scored, with $T_{\mathrm{oos}} = |\mathcal T_{\mathrm{oos}}|$ (its dates and $T_{\mathrm{oos}}$: Experimental
  % setup, Section~\ref{sec:rh_exp_setup})."
\item \textbf{Evaluation set.} Because every forecast is generated by a model estimated on a strictly trailing window, all predictions are out of sample. We let $\mathcal T_{\mathrm{oos}}$ denote the set of common forecast-origin trading days following the initial estimation window and hyperparameter pre-sample on which all competing forecasts are available, excluding early-close days, and write $T_{\mathrm{oos}} = |\mathcal T_{\mathrm{oos}}|$ (the exact sample dates and $T_{\mathrm{oos}}$ are given in Section~\ref{sec:rh_exp_setup}).
  % 2026-10-15 (user): numbers removed -- "(15:30--16:00 ET)" (the closing interval, defined in Problem formulation)
  % and "T_oos = \rhrDays trading days"; the clause "in the HAC formulas, t - j is the out-of-sample day j days
  % before t" is covered by Statistical tests ("numbered t = 1, ..., |O| in time order").
% 2026-10-16 (user): the scoring-loss display moved here from Problem formulation. Was: "The daily QLIKE loss
% $L_t = \QLIKE(\RV_t, \hat\sigma^2_t)$ \citep{Patton2011}, defined in Section~\ref{sec:rh_methods_setup}, and its
% average over $\mathcal T_{\mathrm{oos}}$" (the "over $T_{\mathrm{oos}}$" wording was parked 2026-10-15).
\item \textbf{Primary loss criterion.} Our primary statistical loss is the daily quasi-likelihood (QLIKE) loss of \citet{Patton2011}, which is robust to measurement noise in the realized-variance proxy.
  % 2026-10-16 (user): parked -- Related work no longer surveys forecast evaluation. The cite stays on this loss.
  % Was: "... proxy (Section~\ref{sec:rh_related})."
  Because the diurnal factor $s^2_{t,c}$ cancels between $\RV_t = y_t^2 s^2_{t,c}$ (with unwinsorized $y_t$) and $\hat\sigma^2_t = \hat y_t^{\,2} s^2_{t,c}$, the daily loss takes the form
  \[
    L_t = \frac{y_t^2}{\hat y_t^{\,2}} - \log\frac{y_t^2}{\hat y_t^{\,2}} - 1
      = \QLIKE\bigl(\RV_t,\, \hat\sigma^2_t\bigr),
  \]
  with sample average over $\mathcal T_{\mathrm{oos}}$ given by
  \[
  \overline{\QLIKE} = \bar L = \frac{1}{T_{\mathrm{oos}}}\sum_{t \in \mathcal T_{\mathrm{oos}}} L_t .
  \]
