with a rolling window of \rhorWindowDays{} trading days and evaluate the forecast issued at 15:30 for the closing
half hour over the out-of-sample period from
% 2026-10-20 (user): parked -- Table 1 scored from 2012 (TABLE1_SWITCH.md, approved). Was: "\rhoosSpanStart{} to \rhoosSpanEnd{} ($T_{\mathrm{oos}} = \rhoosDays$ trading days). We compare a benchmark"
\rhoosSpanStart{} to \rhoosSpanEnd{} ($T_{\mathrm{oos}} = \rhoosDays$ trading days), and the forecasts of every
regular-hours bar from \rhorSpanStart{} to \rhorSpanEnd{} (\rhorDays{} trading days). We compare a benchmark
heterogeneous autoregressive (HAR) predictor set against an augmented predictor set that incorporates exogenous
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- "lower ... across all" no longer holds with the pooled linear models on the trees' design; the count is printed. Was: "series. Under the QLIKE loss, the full predictor set yields lower average out-of-sample loss than the HAR benchmark across all \rhrAfPoolPairs{} intraday model classes, with \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level."
series. Under the QLIKE loss, the full predictor set yields a lower average out-of-sample loss than the HAR
benchmark in \rhrAfPoolLower{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} intraday model classes, with \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} of these
% 2026-10-19 (user): parked -- the MSE clause, with the QLIKE/squared-error divergence bullet of 06_results.tex. Was:
% "\rhrAfPoolPairs{} reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level, whereas mean squared error does not
% confirm these gains. Furthermore, ..."
reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level. Furthermore, although every intraday
specification remains less accurate under QLIKE than a
% 2026-10-18 (user, new-notes 11): parked -- a HAR-type model with its horizons, not a daily HAR regression; still the closing-interval OLS the comparison uses (make_rehaul_results.py:1276). Was: "daily HAR regression estimated solely on the closing interval, incorporating the exogenous predictors narrows"
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- "narrows this gap across all model classes"; the count is printed. Was: "... incorporating the exogenous predictors narrows this gap across all model classes."
HAR-type model in the spirit of \citet{Corsi2009}, estimated by OLS on the closing-interval observations of the target's trailing averages over $k \in \{1, 5, 25, 125, 625, 3125\}$ samples plus calendar columns, incorporating the exogenous predictors narrows
this gap in \rhrVsBmPoolNarrow{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} model classes. In an economic application to zero-days-to-expiration (0DTE) S\&P 500
straddles on expiration days, a sign-based trading strategy achieves a higher Sharpe ratio under the full
predictor set than under the HAR benchmark across every intraday model class.
\end{abstract}

