% 09_conclusion.tex -- the rehauled paper's section, as bullets to expand into prose (2026-10-15).
% Sources and pointers behind every number: writeup/PAPER_REHAUL_OUTLINE_2026-10-15.md, section 9.
% House rules: every number in the final prose comes from a generated macro (writeup/generated/*.tex), never
% typed; park (comment out with a dated note) rather than delete.
% Macros to use here: reuse the introduction's macros; no new numbers
% 2026-10-15 (notation pass, writeup/NOTATION_GUIDE_2026-10-15.md): symbols and wording; the typed 866 -> \cmNdays.
% Old -> new: RV_t -> \RV_t; B_t -> s^2_{t,c}; IV^{30}_t -> \mathrm{IV}_t; HAR + calendar -> HAR benchmark;
% exogenous inputs -> exogenous predictors; per bar / pooled -> closing-interval / pooled intraday model;
% Sharpe -> \SR; trade -> straddle strategy; always-short -> short-straddle benchmark; crossing the spread -> at
% quoted bid/ask prices. The guarded \input is a no-op after the introduction's.
% 2026-10-15 (consolidation): window row s -> t' (s is the periodicity factor); the training loss written with the
% realization floor and log-ratio clamp of Methods (FLAG (3) there).
% 2026-10-15 (consolidation): parked -- the number files are loaded once in the preamble of paper_rehaul.tex.
% \ifdefined\cmNdays\else\input{generated/close_main_numbers}\fi

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:rh_conclusion}
\begin{itemize}
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- "across all" no longer holds with the pooled linear models on the trees' design; the count is printed and the heading no longer states a reduction. Was: "\item \textbf{Exogenous predictors reduce QLIKE loss in pooled intraday models.} Relative to the HAR benchmark, augmenting the model with the full exogenous predictor set lowers mean QLIKE across all \rhrAfPoolPairs{} pooled intraday model classes, with \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} of these reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level (Section~\ref{sec:rh_results})."
\item \textbf{Exogenous predictors and QLIKE loss in pooled intraday models.} Relative to the HAR benchmark, augmenting the model with the full exogenous predictor set lowers mean QLIKE in \rhrAfPoolLower{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} pooled intraday model classes, with \rhrAfPoolLowerSig{} of these reductions statistically significant at the 5\% level (Section~\ref{sec:rh_results}).
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34): parked -- with the base-5 pooled trees their reductions are no longer the largest or significant (make_rehaul_results.py, E34 CLAIM); no ranking. Was: "The largest relative reductions occur in the tree ensembles, while among the penalized linear specifications the elastic net achieves a statistically significant reduction."
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- the pooled elastic net's reduction is no longer significant; the count is printed. Was: "Among the penalized linear specifications the elastic net achieves a statistically significant reduction; among the tree ensembles, the reduction is statistically significant at the 5\% level in \rhrAfPoolTreeLowerSig{} of the three classes."
The reduction is statistically significant at the 5\% level in \rhrAfPoolLinLowerSig{} of the three penalized linear classes and in \rhrAfPoolTreeLowerSig{} of the three tree classes.
% 2026-10-19 (user): parked -- the MSE finding, with the QLIKE/squared-error divergence bullet of 06_results.tex and the abstract's MSE clause. Was:
% \item \textbf{Divergence between QLIKE and squared-error loss.} Evaluated under mean squared error, expanding to the full predictor set increases MSE in \rhrAfPoolMseHigher{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} intraday classes, although none of the MSE differences is statistically significant.
% 2026-10-17 (user): parked -- closing-interval material (relabeled: closing-interval HAR benchmark). Was: "Comparison with the daily HAR benchmark. ... than the daily OLS HAR benchmark estimated solely on closing-interval data."
% 2026-10-18 (user, new-notes 10): parked -- closing cadence only in the closing section. Was: "\item \textbf{Comparison with the closing-interval HAR benchmark.} Every pooled intraday specification incurs a higher mean QLIKE than the closing-interval HAR benchmark, the daily OLS HAR regression estimated solely on closing-interval data. Incorporating the full exogenous predictor set narrows this loss gap in every model class, leaving the gap statistically insignificant at the 5\% level for all three penalized linear models while remaining significant for the tree ensembles (Section~\ref{sec:rh_results_classes})."
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34): parked -- with the base-5 pooled trees the gap stays significant for \rhrVsBmPoolTreeFullSig{} of the three tree ensembles, not all (make_rehaul_results.py, E34 CLAIM). Was: "... leaving the gap statistically insignificant at the 5\% level for all three penalized linear models while remaining significant for the tree ensembles (Section~\ref{sec:rh_results_classes})."
\item \textbf{Comparison with the closing-interval HAR benchmark.} Every pooled intraday specification incurs a higher mean QLIKE than the closing-interval HAR benchmark, the OLS HAR regression estimated solely on closing-interval data.
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- "in every model class" and "insignificant ... for all three penalized linear models" no longer hold; counts printed. Was: "Incorporating the full exogenous predictor set narrows this loss gap in every model class, leaving the gap statistically insignificant at the 5\% level for all three penalized linear models while it remains significant for \rhrVsBmPoolTreeFullSig{} of the three tree ensembles (Section~\ref{sec:rh_results_classes})."
Incorporating the full exogenous predictor set narrows this loss gap in \rhrVsBmPoolNarrow{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} model classes; on the full predictor set the gap is statistically significant at the 5\% level for \rhrVsBmPoolLinFullSig{} of the three penalized linear models and for \rhrVsBmPoolTreeFullSig{} of the three tree ensembles (Section~\ref{sec:rh_results_classes}).
% 2026-10-17 (user): parked -- closing-interval material (moved to Appendix sec:rh_econ_last30). Was:
% \item \textbf{Closing-interval estimation as a structural refinement.} Restricting estimation of each model class to the closing half hour lowers mean QLIKE further across all specifications, and within this closing-interval scheme the full exogenous predictor set continues to reduce mean QLIKE in every model class (Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ_last30}).
\item \textbf{Models trained on the closing half hour.} Results for each model class estimated on the closing interval alone are reported in Appendix~\ref{sec:rh_econ_last30}.
% 2026-10-17 (user, E25): parked -- the rank correlations now run over the pooled intraday forecasts (\rheRankCorrQPool, \rheRankCorrESPool), where QLIKE is no longer weakly correlated. Was: "Across the full model set, global statistical accuracy ($\overline{\QLIKE}$) exhibits only a weak rank correlation with straddle Sharpe ratios, whereas local accuracy at the implied-variance threshold ($\overline{\mathrm{ES}}_1$) aligns much more closely with economic gains"
% 2026-10-18 (user, second-list N5): parked -- the correlation is between loss and SR, not accuracy and SR; values from \rheRankCorrQPool / \rheRankCorrESPool. Was: "\item \textbf{Economic value in 0DTE straddles.} When the variance forecasts are used to trade same-day-expiring SPXW straddles over the closing half hour on the option-data subsample, adding the exogenous predictors increases the sign strategy's post-warmup Sharpe ratio in every intraday model class. Across the \rhrPoolN{} pooled intraday forecasts, both global statistical accuracy ($\overline{\QLIKE}$) and local accuracy at the implied-variance threshold ($\overline{\mathrm{ES}}_1$) are negatively rank-correlated with straddle Sharpe ratios (Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ_results})."
\item \textbf{Economic value in 0DTE straddles.} When the variance forecasts are used to trade same-day-expiring SPXW straddles over the closing half hour on the option-data subsample, adding the exogenous predictors increases the sign strategy's post-warmup Sharpe ratio in every intraday model class. % 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- the sign claim ("both negatively rank-correlated"), which does not hold on one design. Was: "Across the \rhrPoolN{} pooled intraday forecasts, average QLIKE loss and $\overline{\mathrm{ES}}_1$, the loss at the implied-variance threshold, are both negatively rank-correlated with straddle Sharpe ratios, at \rheRankCorrQPool{} and a weak \rheRankCorrESPool{} for $\overline{\mathrm{ES}}_1$ (Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ_results})."
Across the \rhrPoolN{} pooled intraday forecasts, the rank correlation of the straddle Sharpe ratio is \rheRankCorrQPool{} with average QLIKE loss and \rheRankCorrESPool{} with $\overline{\mathrm{ES}}_1$, the loss at the implied-variance threshold (Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ_results}).
% 2026-10-22 (user, E34: one design for the 12 pooled forecasts): parked -- the note named one class. Was: "\editc{orange}{N15c}{Problem: ``every intraday model class'' no longer holds under the per-year Sharpe ratio (pooled random forest $\Delta\SR = \rhrAfRfPoolDSh$); see note N15c in Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ}.}"
% 2026-10-22 (user, D6): parked -- the note under the per-year ratio D1; under the Sharpe ratio of note D6 the sentence holds in point estimates. Was: "\editc{orange}{N15c}{Problem: ``every intraday model class'' does not hold: the full predictor set raises the pooled Sharpe ratio in \rhrAfPoolShHigher{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} classes ($\Delta\SR$ from \rhrAfPoolDShMin{} to \rhrAfPoolDShMax); see note N15c in Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ}.}"
\editc{orange}{N15c}{Problem: under the Sharpe ratio of note D6, the full predictor set raises the pooled Sharpe ratio in \rhrAfPoolShHigher{} of the \rhrAfPoolPairs{} classes ($\Delta\SR$ from \rhrAfPoolDShMin{} to \rhrAfPoolDShMax), so ``every intraday model class'' holds in point estimates; \rhrAfPoolShHigherSig{} of these gains are statistically significant at the 5\% level (bootstrap test), and under the previous ratio the count was \rheDefDOnePos{} of \rheDefN{}. See notes N15c and D6 in Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ}.}
\item \textbf{Scope and limitations.}
  \begin{itemize}
  % 2026-10-20 (user): parked -- Table 1 scored from 2012 (TABLE1_SWITCH.md, approved). Was: "\item Our out-of-sample evaluation spans $T_{\mathrm{oos}} = \rhoosDays$ trading days from \rhoosSpanStart{} to \rhoosSpanEnd."
  % 2026-10-22 (user, E36): parked -- "the forecast accuracy of the pooled linear models (Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos})"; Table 1 now holds the tree ensembles too.
  \item Our out-of-sample evaluation spans $T_{\mathrm{oos}} = \rhoosDays$ trading days from \rhoosSpanStart{} to \rhoosSpanEnd; the forecast accuracy of the pooled linear models and tree ensembles (Table~\ref{tab:rh_oos}) is scored on \rhorDays{} trading days from \rhorSpanStart{} to \rhorSpanEnd.
  \item The empirical analysis focuses on a single equity index (the S\&P~500) and a single target horizon (the 15:30--16:00 ET closing half hour).
  \item In our economic evaluation, the straddle is held unhedged to cash settlement, and executing at quoted bid and ask prices rather than mid-quotes materially reduces the strategy's Sharpe ratio (Section~\ref{sec:rh_econ}).
  \end{itemize}
\item \textbf{Directions for future work.} Natural extensions include out-of-sample evaluation on post-\rhoosSpanEnd{} option expirations, cross-market validation on additional index option chains, and intraday variance forecasting at earlier decision origins prior to 15:30 ET.
\end{itemize}
